            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to spin, then the Latin for wool-working."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: rūs, rūris",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rūs, rūris — \"the countryside, the country; a farm\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Aestāte multī cīvēs rūs eunt. In summer many citizens go to the country."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third declension, neuter: rūs, rūris n. Like domus, it takes no preposition: rūs 'to the country', rūre 'from the country', rūrī 'in the country'. English rural and rustic come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "rūs is inherited from Proto-Italic rowos, from PIE rewh₁- 'open space, field' (EtymDB). English rural and, through rūsticus, rustic come from it; German Raum 'space' is a cousin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
